{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-338513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+4y(x)=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nWhich of the following best classifies the boundary conditions for this problem?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Periodic:\n\n$$\ny(a)=y(b),\\,{y}^{′}(a)={y}^{′}(b)\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following boundary conditions, defined on $x=a=0$ and $x=b=1.$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nThese are Dirichlet boundary conditions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "817d1cc9029fd82cb73c2ef68d01bf30890c9c96a7a4148c1516ed662e00eb80"} {"question" "q-338513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Dirichlet"] ["text" "Neumann"] ["text" "Other"] ["text" "Periodic"] ["text" "Robin"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "Dirichlet"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi \n$$\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}(x)+y(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)=2x≢0,$ the equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\n2y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\pi )+2{y}^{′}(\\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Robin}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is inhomogeneous, yet the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "817d1cc9029fd82cb73c2ef68d01bf30890c9c96a7a4148c1516ed662e00eb80"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "homogeneous"] ["text" "inhomogeneous"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338516" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "Dirichlet"] ["text" "Neumann"] ["text" "Robin"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"qu