 "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338646" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "homogeneous"] ["text" "inhomogeneous"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338646" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "inhomogeneous"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/state.json" "file_sha256" "6962f08dcbe9f321c5d710c8aa91047299c39ee2805f268d1be7d70ac83ed09f"} {"question" "q-338695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069836/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "817d1cc9029fd82cb73c2ef68d01bf30890c9c96a7a